"Yonkoma Guidelines.cdr (https://www.uk.emb-japan.go.jp/files/100069624.pdf)\nciteturn0view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 1; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.uk.emb-japan.go.jp/files/100069624.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 0\nThis page has no readable lines.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nejcjs - The Functions of Panels (koma) in Manga: An essay by Natsume Fusanosuke (https://japanesestudies.org.uk/ejcjs/vol21/iss2/holt_fukuda.html)\nciteturn0view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://japanesestudies.org.uk/ejcjs/vol21/iss2/holt_fukuda.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 230\nL0: # The Functions of Panels (koma) in Manga: An essay by Natsume Fusanosuke\nL1: \nL2: #### translated by Jon Holt and Teppei Fukuda\nL3: \nL4: Jon Holt, 【0†Portland State University†www.pdx.edu】 [【1†About】 | Email (joholt@pdx.edu)]\nL5: \nL6: Teppei Fukuda, 【0†Portland State University†www.pdx.edu】 [【1†About】 | Email (tfukuda@uoregon.edu)]\nL7: \nL8: 【2†Volume 21, Issue 2】 (Translation 2 in 2021). First published in ejcjs on 16 August 2021.\nL9: ## Abstract\nL10: Natsume Fusanosuke presents in this essay the core ideas of his formal ‘theory of expression’ (manga hyōgen-ron) that focuses on three basic elements of manga: words, pictures, and frames. In the 1990s, Natsume emerged as a seminal scholar of Manga Studies, whose influential works include Manga no yomikata (coauthored, 1995) and Manga wa naze omoshiroi no ka: sono hyōgen to bunpō (1997), where the present essay is found.\nL11: Here, Natsume describes the central aspects of panel constructions in manga: creating a sense of order for the reader by segmenting time; shaping the reader’s mental perceptions by panel compression (asshuku) and release (kaihō); and, making a symbolic space within the frame.\nL12: For Natsume, manga artists in the 1960s, most notably Ishinomori Shōtarō, pioneered these techniques during this seminal and creative period of manga, effectively establishing the techniques that all manga artists have used since then.\nL13: In this culminating chapter from his groundbreaking 1997 work, Natsume describes how these artists made manga more ‘interesting’ (omoshiroi) by transforming and leveraging the formal aspects of the manga page and the layout of panels in order to both generate new psychological effects and greater reader involvement with the story’s characters and its mood.\nL14: Keywords: manga, pop culture, panel constructions, asshuku, kaihō, Ishinomori Shōtarō, Bon Bon, manga techniques, Natsume Fusanosuke\nL15: ### 1. Translator's Introduction\nL16: Natsume Fusanosuke (1950- ), a leader in manga studies since the 1990s, really needs no introduction for scholars of contemporary Japanese culture. Author, comic-book artist, columnist, critic, scholar, educator, television personality—he is well known by all of these titles—not to mention that he is also the grandson of Natsume Sōseki (1867-1916), the great Meiji novelist.\nL17: Beginning with cartoons and comics for magazines like the Weekly Asahi in the 1970s, by the 1990s Natsume went from making manga to writing about manga. From his early experiments in manga criticism, such as his idiosyncratic but very enjoyable Mangagaku (Yamato Shobō, 1985), Natsume went on to trailblaze a new approach to manga scholarship with Manga no yomikata ([How to Read Manga], Takarajima-sha, 1995), a co-authored work but one that largely consists of Natsume’s ground-breaking contributions.\nL18: Within a year after that, he developed those ideas further for the NHK education television show Ningen daikgaku (Human University), which was broadcast from July 4 to September 23, 1996. His twelve-episode weekly series, Manga wa naze omoshiroi no ka: sono bunpō to hyōgen (Why Is Manga So Interesting?: Its Grammar and Expression), was accompanied by a reader’s guide, which he later expanded and re-published in the following year under the same title (NHK Library, 1997).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nマンガ賞一覧 | 集英社主催の賞 | 集英社　―　SHUEISHA　― (https://www.shueisha.co.jp/company/award/comics/)\nciteturn0view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.shueisha.co.jp/company/award/comics/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 237\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: MENU\nL3: \nL4: [Input: 本をさがす] [Button: 検索]\nL5: \nL6:   * 企業情報\nL7: \nL8:     * 【1†会社概要】\nL9:     * 【2†トップメッセージ】\nL10:     * 【3†集英社小史】\nL11:     * 【4†さまざまな取り組み】\nL12:     * 【5†100周年記念企画ガイド】\nL13:     * 【6†集英社主催の賞】\nL14:     * 【7†関係会社・団体一覧】\nL15:     * 【8†アクセス】\nL16: \nL17: 【9†企業情報TOPへ】\nL18: \nL19:   * ニュース\nL20: \nL21:     * 【10†お知らせ】\nL22:     * 【11†メディア化】\nL23:     * 【12†イベント・キャンペーン】\nL24:     * 【13†受賞関連】\nL25:     * 【14†プレスリリース】\nL26: \nL27: 【15†ニュース一覧へ】\nL28: \nL29:   * 採用情報\nL30: \nL31:     * 【16†定期採用】\nL32:     * 【17†経験者採用】\nL33:     * 【18†アルバイト募集】\nL34: \nL35:   * 商品&サービス一覧\nL36:     * 【19†雑誌】\nL37:     * 【19†書籍】\nL38:     * 【19†コミックス】\nL39:     * 【19†公式サイト】\nL40:     * 【20†イベントカレンダー】\nL41:     * 【19†オンラインストア】\nL42: \nL43:   * 【5†100周年記念企画ガイド】\nL44: \nL45:   * 【21†お問い合わせはこちら】\nL46:   * [Input: 本をさがす] [Button: 検索]\nL47: \nL48:   * 【22†著作権†faq.shueisha.co.jp】\nL49:   * 【23†広告掲載†adnavi.shueisha.co.jp】\nL50:   * 【24†サイトマップ】\nL51:   * [Select]\nL52: # 企業情報\nL53: \nL54: ## 集英社主催の賞\nL55: \nL56: ### 【25†Image: マンガ賞】 【26†Image: マンガ賞】\nL57: \nL58: 各マンガ誌毎に漫画家を目指している方の作品投稿を受け付けております。あなたの力作をふるってご応募ください。\nL59: \nL60:   * 【27†少年マンガ】\nL61:   * 【28†青年マンガ】\nL62:   * 【29†少女マンガ】\nL63:   * 【30†女性マンガ】\nL64: #### 少年マンガ\nL65: \nL66: 手塚賞・赤塚賞\nL67: \nL68: 【31†Image: 手塚賞・赤塚賞】\nL69: \nL70:   * 【32†詳細はこちら†www.jump-mangasho.com】\nL71: \nL72: 週刊少年ジャンプ 原稿持ち込み\nL73: （03-3230-6233）\nL74: \nL75: 【33†Image: 週刊少年ジャンプ】\nL76: \nL77:   * 【34†漫画賞ポータルサイト†www.jump-mangasho.com】\nL78: 